.Dd July 5, 2026
.Dt XTC_STACK_RECLAIM 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_stack_reclaim_enable ,
.Nm xtc_stack_reclaim_disable ,
.Nm xtc_stack_reclaim_enabled ,
.Nm xtc_stack_reclaim_count
.Nd reclaim a parked fiber's unused stack memory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_async.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_enable "size_t keep_bytes"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_disable "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_enabled "void"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_count "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A parked stackful fiber commits only the pages it has touched, but its
mmap'd stack reserves the full configured size
.Pq Xr xtc_set_stack_size 3 .
With reclamation enabled, a fiber that parks
.Pq via Fn xtc_yield , which the receive, latch, and timer park paths route through
returns the unused tail of its stack -- the region below its current
stack pointer, beyond a small live margin, page-aligned -- to the
operating system with
.Fn madvise MADV_DONTNEED .
The pages fault back as zero-fill on resume.
This lowers the per-parked-fiber memory floor for the many-idle-fiber
case.
It is predictable: no relocation, no garbage collection, no
segmented-stack thrash.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_enable
turns reclamation on process-wide;
.Fa keep_bytes
is the live margin left mapped below the saved stack pointer, and
.Li 0
selects a one-page default.
Reclamation fires only when the reclaimable span exceeds a page, so
shallow, frequently-woken fibers are not churned.
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_disable
turns it off;
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_enabled
reports the current state; and
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_count
reports how many reclaim calls have been made (telemetry).
Reclamation is OFF by default: the cooperative fast path is unchanged
unless it is enabled.
.Sh SCOPE
The reclaimed region is exactly
.Bq stack_base , park_SP :
the portion below the parked fiber's stack pointer (stacks grow down, so
that is the not-yet-descended-into region).
The benefit is realized for a fiber whose committed stack high-water
sits below where it parks -- for example a handler that recurses deep
during parsing or planning, returns, then parks shallow awaiting I/O.
A fiber that parks at its deepest point has little below its stack
pointer to reclaim; the pages between the park point and the stack top
are live and are not reclaimed while parked.
For extreme fan-in where even that floor is too much, the stackless
Isolate layer
.Pq Xr xtc_tnt 3
is the better tool; the framework offers both models deliberately.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_stack_reclaim_enable
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
where
.Fn madvise MADV_DONTNEED
is unavailable (for example Windows), where reclamation is a documented
no-op.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_async 3 ,
.Xr xtc_set_stack_size 3 ,
.Xr xtc_tnt 3
